{"v":1,"dev":"01a077b6-4c36-77bc-879a-5f58767d5cc4","seq":1004,"lc":1004,"ts":1789833142223,"enc":"none","op":"block.set","d":{"pageId":"01a0ba47-8062-73ae-8e5f-d3d4f08eae3c","block":{"absorbedIds":[],"access":null,"content":{"ink":{"add":[],"base":"749cd75058b5194201264c0118521d50bdfa53efc7ca27419ff5df6ee2a78cea","gone":"","n":25,"o":[55.23741662549407,2839.5578082222937],"v":1}},"createdAt":1789833133236,"frameId":null,"h":183.0882257956605,"id":"01a0ba5e-3cb4-70b2-9a44-7cf328162277","placement":"snapped","rotation":0.0,"type":"ink","updatedAt":1789833141502,"w":363.5882122859025,"x":55.23741662549407,"y":2839.5578082222937,"z":78}}}
{"v":1,"dev":"01a077b6-4c36-77bc-879a-5f58767d5cc4","seq":1005,"lc":1005,"ts":1789833146936,"enc":"none","op":"block.set","d":{"pageId":"01a0ba47-8062-73ae-8e5f-d3d4f08eae3c","block":{"absorbedIds":[],"access":null,"content":{"text":""},"createdAt":1789833146211,"frameId":null,"h":null,"id":"01a0ba5e-6f63-773b-b3b1-de0b65747159","placement":"snapped","rotation":0.0,"type":"text","updatedAt":1789833146211,"w":200.0,"x":192.0,"y":3048.0,"z":79}}}
{"v":1,"dev":"01a077b6-4c36-77bc-879a-5f58767d5cc4","seq":1006,"lc":1006,"ts":1789833148176,"enc":"none","op":"block.set","d":{"pageId":"01a0ba47-8062-73ae-8e5f-d3d4f08eae3c","block":{"absorbedIds":[],"access":null,"content":{"text":"As an enterprise cybersecurity trainer, here are three common risks in banking IT infrastructure, along with practical examples, business impact, and mitigations.\n\n| Risk | Practical Example | Business Impact | Mitigation 1 | Mitigation 2 |\n|------|-------------------|-----------------|--------------|--------------|\n| **Phishing / Credential Theft** | An employee clicks a lookalike email that mimics the bank's internal portal, entering their domain password on a fake login page. | Account takeover, unauthorized fund transfers, data breach, regulatory fines, and loss of customer trust. | Enforce phishing-resistant multi-factor authentication (e.g., FIDO2 security keys) for all staff and privileged users. | Conduct regular simulated phishing drills and provide immediate feedback training for those who fail. |\n| **Ransomware / Malware** | A bank branch's file server is encrypted by malware that entered through a compromised third-party vendor's remote-access tool. | Service outage, halted transactions, forensic investigation costs, potential ransom payment, and reputational damage. | Maintain offline, immutable backups and test restoration procedures regularly to ensure recovery without paying. | Segment the network so endpoint infections cannot spread to core banking systems; restrict administrative access. |\n| **Unpatched / Vulnerable Systems** | A public-facing API gateway runs an outdated version of a web server with a known critical CVE, which an attacker exploits to exfiltrate customer records. | Data breach, exposure of sensitive financial data, legal liability, and non-compliance with PCI-DSS or other regulations. | Implement an automated patch-management process with a defined SLA for critical vulnerabilities (e.g., patch within 48 hours). | Use a web application firewall (WAF) and runtime protection to block exploitation attempts while patches are deployed. |\n\nThese are grounded in common industry findings and standard cybersecurity frameworks (e.g., OWASP, NIST). For your operations team, the key is consistent monitoring, quick patching, and assuming a breach will happen — so you can detect and respond fast."},"createdAt":1789833146211,"frameId":null,"h":null,"id":"01a0ba5e-6f63-773b-b3b1-de0b65747159","placement":"snapped","rotation":0.0,"type":"text","updatedAt":1789833147457,"w":640.0,"x":192.0,"y":3048.0,"z":79}}}
