{"v":1,"dev":"01a077b6-4c36-77bc-879a-5f58767d5cc4","seq":1159,"lc":1159,"ts":1789833783711,"enc":"none","op":"block.set","d":{"pageId":"01a0ba47-8062-73ae-8e5f-d3d4f08eae3c","block":{"absorbedIds":[],"access":null,"content":{"autoWidth":false,"pasted":true,"text":"When a system is down, work through it calmly and systematically:\n\n1. **Confirm the outage** – Check if it's just you or everyone (ask a colleague, check a status page or monitoring tool).\n2. **Communicate** – Tell your team/manager and log an incident ticket. Don't go quiet.\n3. **Diagnose** – Look at logs, recent deployments, config changes, or connectivity issues. Most outages have a recent trigger.\n4. **Apply known fixes** – Restart the service, fail over, or roll back the last change if that's the likely cause.\n5. **Escalate** – If you can't fix it quickly, involve the on-call team or vendor per your escalation policy.\n6. **Verify & document** – Confirm the system is healthy, then note the root cause and prevention steps.\n\n**Golden rule:** fix the service first, find the root cause second. Keep stakeholders informed the whole time."},"createdAt":1789833782969,"frameId":null,"h":null,"id":"01a0ba68-26b9-7c26-9d77-238c7fd8527e","placement":"snapped","rotation":0.0,"type":"text","updatedAt":1789833782969,"w":464.0,"x":504.0,"y":4512.0,"z":125}}}
{"v":1,"dev":"01a077b6-4c36-77bc-879a-5f58767d5cc4","seq":1160,"lc":1160,"ts":1789833786235,"enc":"none","op":"block.set","d":{"pageId":"01a0ba47-8062-73ae-8e5f-d3d4f08eae3c","block":{"absorbedIds":[],"access":null,"content":{"autoWidth":false,"pasted":true,"text":"When a system is down, work through it calmly and systematically:\n\n1. **Confirm the outage** – Check if it's just you or everyone (ask a colleague, check a status page or monitoring tool).\n2. **Communicate** – Tell your team/manager and log an incident ticket. Don't go quiet.\n3. **Diagnose** – Look at logs, recent deployments, config changes, or connectivity issues. Most outages have a recent trigger.\n4. **Apply known fixes** – Restart the service, fail over, or roll back the last change if that's the likely cause.\n5. **Escalate** – If you can't fix it quickly, involve the on-call team or vendor per your escalation policy.\n6. **Verify & document** – Confirm the system is healthy, then note the root cause and prevention steps.\n\n**Golden rule:** fix the service first, find the root cause second. Keep stakeholders informed the whole time."},"createdAt":1789833782969,"frameId":null,"h":null,"id":"01a0ba68-26b9-7c26-9d77-238c7fd8527e","placement":"snapped","rotation":0.0,"type":"text","updatedAt":1789833782969,"w":464.0,"x":216.0,"y":4560.0,"z":125}}}
