{"v":1,"dev":"01a077b6-4c36-77bc-879a-5f58767d5cc4","seq":1355,"lc":1355,"ts":1789834450122,"enc":"none","op":"block.set","d":{"pageId":"01a0ba47-8062-73ae-8e5f-d3d4f08eae3c","block":{"absorbedIds":[],"access":null,"content":{"pasted":true,"text":"Explain cybersecurity."},"createdAt":1789834434195,"frameId":null,"h":null,"id":"01a0ba72-1693-7011-a5e3-c6bcc7593d0a","placement":"snapped","rotation":0.0,"type":"text","updatedAt":1789834439881,"w":205.83544921875,"x":120.0,"y":7944.0,"z":177}}}
{"v":1,"dev":"01a077b6-4c36-77bc-879a-5f58767d5cc4","seq":1356,"lc":1356,"ts":1789834462788,"enc":"none","op":"block.set","d":{"pageId":"01a0ba47-8062-73ae-8e5f-d3d4f08eae3c","block":{"absorbedIds":[],"access":null,"content":{"autoWidth":false,"pasted":true,"text":"Cybersecurity is the practice of protecting systems, networks, programs, and data from digital attacks, theft, or damage. \n\nThink of it as **digital defense** for anything connected to a computer or the internet. It generally focuses on three core goals, often called the **CIA triad**:\n\n- **Confidentiality** – Keeping sensitive data private (only authorized people can see it).\n- **Integrity** – Ensuring data isn’t tampered with or altered without permission.\n- **Availability** – Making sure systems and data are accessible when needed (e.g., preventing a website from crashing or going offline).\n\nIt covers areas like protecting endpoints (laptops/phones), securing networks, managing user access (passwords/MFA), detecting threats (monitoring), and responding to incidents (like the system outage you asked about earlier). In your banking context, cybersecurity is the umbrella that includes everything we discussed — authentication, encryption, fraud detection, and more."},"createdAt":1789834462035,"frameId":null,"h":null,"id":"01a0ba72-8353-7f83-b972-31a9bcb76ae7","placement":"snapped","rotation":0.0,"type":"text","updatedAt":1789834462035,"w":464.0,"x":504.0,"y":7968.0,"z":178}}}
{"v":1,"dev":"01a077b6-4c36-77bc-879a-5f58767d5cc4","seq":1357,"lc":1357,"ts":1789834464705,"enc":"none","op":"block.set","d":{"pageId":"01a0ba47-8062-73ae-8e5f-d3d4f08eae3c","block":{"absorbedIds":[],"access":null,"content":{"autoWidth":false,"pasted":true,"text":"Cybersecurity is the practice of protecting systems, networks, programs, and data from digital attacks, theft, or damage. \n\nThink of it as **digital defense** for anything connected to a computer or the internet. It generally focuses on three core goals, often called the **CIA triad**:\n\n- **Confidentiality** – Keeping sensitive data private (only authorized people can see it).\n- **Integrity** – Ensuring data isn’t tampered with or altered without permission.\n- **Availability** – Making sure systems and data are accessible when needed (e.g., preventing a website from crashing or going offline).\n\nIt covers areas like protecting endpoints (laptops/phones), securing networks, managing user access (passwords/MFA), detecting threats (monitoring), and responding to incidents (like the system outage you asked about earlier). In your banking context, cybersecurity is the umbrella that includes everything we discussed — authentication, encryption, fraud detection, and more."},"createdAt":1789834462035,"frameId":null,"h":null,"id":"01a0ba72-8353-7f83-b972-31a9bcb76ae7","placement":"snapped","rotation":0.0,"type":"text","updatedAt":1789834462035,"w":464.0,"x":264.0,"y":8040.0,"z":178}}}
{"v":1,"dev":"01a077b6-4c36-77bc-879a-5f58767d5cc4","seq":1358,"lc":1358,"ts":1789834476444,"enc":"none","op":"block.set","d":{"pageId":"01a0ba47-8062-73ae-8e5f-d3d4f08eae3c","block":{"absorbedIds":[],"access":null,"content":{"autoWidth":false,"pasted":true,"text":"You are a cybersecurity awareness trainer.\n\nTask: Explain phishing attacks to new corporate employees.\n\nContext: The audience is nontechnical and uses email daily.\n\nRequirements:\n- Use simple language.\n- Explain three common examples.\n- Include five warning signs.\n- End with three practical safety tips.\n\nOutput format:\nUse headings and bullet points.\nKeep the explanation under 400 words."},"createdAt":1789834475705,"frameId":null,"h":null,"id":"01a0ba72-b8b9-7aab-9d38-48c07c1cb2c4","placement":"snapped","rotation":0.0,"type":"text","updatedAt":1789834475705,"w":464.0,"x":504.0,"y":8664.0,"z":179}}}
{"v":1,"dev":"01a077b6-4c36-77bc-879a-5f58767d5cc4","seq":1359,"lc":1359,"ts":1789834478064,"enc":"none","op":"block.set","d":{"pageId":"01a0ba47-8062-73ae-8e5f-d3d4f08eae3c","block":{"absorbedIds":[],"access":null,"content":{"autoWidth":false,"pasted":true,"text":"You are a cybersecurity awareness trainer.\n\nTask: Explain phishing attacks to new corporate employees.\n\nContext: The audience is nontechnical and uses email daily.\n\nRequirements:\n- Use simple language.\n- Explain three common examples.\n- Include five warning signs.\n- End with three practical safety tips.\n\nOutput format:\nUse headings and bullet points.\nKeep the explanation under 400 words."},"createdAt":1789834475705,"frameId":null,"h":null,"id":"01a0ba72-b8b9-7aab-9d38-48c07c1cb2c4","placement":"snapped","rotation":0.0,"type":"text","updatedAt":1789834475705,"w":464.0,"x":120.0,"y":8664.0,"z":179}}}
{"v":1,"dev":"01a077b6-4c36-77bc-879a-5f58767d5cc4","seq":1360,"lc":1360,"ts":1789834479664,"enc":"none","op":"block.set","d":{"pageId":"01a0ba47-8062-73ae-8e5f-d3d4f08eae3c","block":{"absorbedIds":[],"access":null,"content":{"autoWidth":false,"pasted":true,"text":"You are a cybersecurity awareness trainer.\n\nTask: Explain phishing attacks to new corporate employees.\n\nContext: The audience is nontechnical and uses email daily.\n\nRequirements:\n- Use simple language.\n- Explain three common examples.\n- Include five warning signs.\n- End with three practical safety tips.\n\nOutput format:\nUse headings and bullet points.\nKeep the explanation under 400 words."},"createdAt":1789834475705,"frameId":null,"h":null,"id":"01a0ba72-b8b9-7aab-9d38-48c07c1cb2c4","placement":"snapped","rotation":0.0,"type":"text","updatedAt":1789834475705,"w":464.0,"x":64.0,"y":8640.0,"z":179}}}
{"v":1,"dev":"01a077b6-4c36-77bc-879a-5f58767d5cc4","seq":1361,"lc":1361,"ts":1789834494351,"enc":"none","op":"block.set","d":{"pageId":"01a0ba47-8062-73ae-8e5f-d3d4f08eae3c","block":{"absorbedIds":[],"access":null,"content":{"text":""},"createdAt":1789834493606,"frameId":null,"h":null,"id":"01a0ba72-fea6-7d26-891a-853cb81aa6dd","placement":"snapped","rotation":0.0,"type":"text","updatedAt":1789834493606,"w":200.0,"x":336.0,"y":9048.0,"z":180}}}
{"v":1,"dev":"01a077b6-4c36-77bc-879a-5f58767d5cc4","seq":1362,"lc":1362,"ts":1789834495642,"enc":"none","op":"block.set","d":{"pageId":"01a0ba47-8062-73ae-8e5f-d3d4f08eae3c","block":{"absorbedIds":[],"access":null,"content":{"autoWidth":false,"text":"## Phishing Attacks Explained\n\nPhishing is when a criminal sends you a fake message (usually email) pretending to be someone you trust, trying to trick you into clicking a bad link, downloading a dangerous file, or giving away your password. It's like a digital con artist knocking on your inbox door.\n\n### Three Common Examples\n\n- **Fake \"Password Expired\" email** – Looks like it's from IT, telling you to click a link and \"re-activate\" your account. The link goes to a fake login page that steals your password.\n- **Fake \"Urgent Invoice\"** – Appears to come from a vendor or executive, saying a payment is overdue. It asks you to open an attachment or transfer money quickly.\n- **Fake \"Package Delivery\" notice** – Claims you missed a delivery and asks you to download a tracking label. The attachment installs malware instead.\n\n### Five Warning Signs\n\n1. **Urgency or fear** – \"Act now!\" \"Your account will be locked!\" Attackers want you to rush and not think.\n2. **Unexpected sender** – An email from someone you don't normally hear from, or a slightly off address (e.g., `amaz0n-support.com`).\n3. **Suspicious links** – Hover over a link before clicking; if the destination doesn't match the text, don't click.\n4. **Poor grammar or odd tone** – Typos, awkward phrasing, or a tone that doesn't match how the person normally writes.\n5. **Requests for sensitive info** – Legitimate companies rarely ask for passwords or account numbers by email.\n\n### Three Practical Safety Tips\n\n- **When in doubt, verify** – Contact the person through a known phone number or a new email, not by replying to the suspicious message.\n- **Never reuse passwords** – If a phisher gets one password, use a password manager and enable multi-factor authentication (MFA) to protect your accounts.\n- **Report, don't delete** – Click the \"Report Phishing\" button in your email client so your security team can warn others."},"createdAt":1789834493606,"frameId":null,"h":null,"id":"01a0ba72-fea6-7d26-891a-853cb81aa6dd","placement":"snapped","rotation":0.0,"type":"text","updatedAt":1789834494917,"w":632.0,"x":336.0,"y":9048.0,"z":180}}}
{"v":1,"dev":"01a077b6-4c36-77bc-879a-5f58767d5cc4","seq":1363,"lc":1363,"ts":1789834497053,"enc":"none","op":"block.set","d":{"pageId":"01a0ba47-8062-73ae-8e5f-d3d4f08eae3c","block":{"absorbedIds":[],"access":null,"content":{"autoWidth":false,"text":"## Phishing Attacks Explained\n\nPhishing is when a criminal sends you a fake message (usually email) pretending to be someone you trust, trying to trick you into clicking a bad link, downloading a dangerous file, or giving away your password. It's like a digital con artist knocking on your inbox door.\n\n### Three Common Examples\n\n- **Fake \"Password Expired\" email** – Looks like it's from IT, telling you to click a link and \"re-activate\" your account. The link goes to a fake login page that steals your password.\n- **Fake \"Urgent Invoice\"** – Appears to come from a vendor or executive, saying a payment is overdue. It asks you to open an attachment or transfer money quickly.\n- **Fake \"Package Delivery\" notice** – Claims you missed a delivery and asks you to download a tracking label. The attachment installs malware instead.\n\n### Five Warning Signs\n\n1. **Urgency or fear** – \"Act now!\" \"Your account will be locked!\" Attackers want you to rush and not think.\n2. **Unexpected sender** – An email from someone you don't normally hear from, or a slightly off address (e.g., `amaz0n-support.com`).\n3. **Suspicious links** – Hover over a link before clicking; if the destination doesn't match the text, don't click.\n4. **Poor grammar or odd tone** – Typos, awkward phrasing, or a tone that doesn't match how the person normally writes.\n5. **Requests for sensitive info** – Legitimate companies rarely ask for passwords or account numbers by email.\n\n### Three Practical Safety Tips\n\n- **When in doubt, verify** – Contact the person through a known phone number or a new email, not by replying to the suspicious message.\n- **Never reuse passwords** – If a phisher gets one password, use a password manager and enable multi-factor authentication (MFA) to protect your accounts.\n- **Report, don't delete** – Click the \"Report Phishing\" button in your email client so your security team can warn others."},"createdAt":1789834493606,"frameId":null,"h":null,"id":"01a0ba72-fea6-7d26-891a-853cb81aa6dd","placement":"snapped","rotation":0.0,"type":"text","updatedAt":1789834494917,"w":632.0,"x":192.0,"y":9048.0,"z":180}}}
{"v":1,"dev":"01a077b6-4c36-77bc-879a-5f58767d5cc4","seq":1364,"lc":1364,"ts":1789834504823,"enc":"none","op":"block.set","d":{"pageId":"01a0ba47-8062-73ae-8e5f-d3d4f08eae3c","block":{"absorbedIds":[],"access":null,"content":{"autoWidth":false,"pasted":true,"text":"Cybersecurity is the practice of protecting systems, networks, programs, and data from digital attacks, theft, or damage. \n\nThink of it as **digital defense** for anything connected to a computer or the internet. It generally focuses on three core goals, often called the **CIA triad**:\n\n- **Confidentiality** – Keeping sensitive data private (only authorized people can see it).\n- **Integrity** – Ensuring data isn’t tampered with or altered without permission.\n- **Availability** – Making sure systems and data are accessible when needed (e.g., preventing a website from crashing or going offline).\n\nIt covers areas like protecting endpoints (laptops/phones), securing networks, managing user access (passwords/MFA), detecting threats (monitoring), and responding to incidents (like the system outage you asked about earlier). In your banking context, cybersecurity is the umbrella that includes everything we discussed — authentication, encryption, fraud detection, and more."},"createdAt":1789834462035,"frameId":null,"h":null,"id":"01a0ba72-8353-7f83-b972-31a9bcb76ae7","placement":"snapped","rotation":0.0,"type":"text","updatedAt":1789834504089,"w":596.2523742320959,"x":264.0,"y":8040.0,"z":178}}}
